---
import { OFFICIAL_SOURCES } from '../../config/site';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const FAQ_ITEMS = [
  {
    question: 'Is it still legal to repair and recharge an existing R-22 air conditioner?',
    answer:
      'Yes. While EPA banned virgin R-22 production and import on January 1, 2020, technicians may still service existing systems using recovered, recycled, or reclaimed R-22 stocks—though reclaimed refrigerant carries higher scarcity pricing.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why does Check C require both R-22 refrigerant and age over 12 years?',
    answer:
      'Because new R-22 equipment was banned in 2010, virtually all surviving R-22 systems are well past 12 years old. Combining obsolete refrigerant cost with aging compressors and coils creates high risk of back-to-back major repairs.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I also panic-replace an 8-year-old R-410A unit because of the AIM Act?',
    answer:
      'No. Under the EPA AIM Act, HFC production caps transition new equipment to lower-GWP refrigerants like R-454B and R-32, but existing R-410A units remain serviceable for routine repairs.',
  },
] as const;

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: FAQ_ITEMS.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.question,
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: item.answer,
    },
  })),
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="R-22 Refrigerant Phase-Out: What It Means for Old AC | HVAC Second Opinion"
  description="EPA banned R-22 production and import on Jan 1, 2020. Learn how reclaimed R-22, the AIM Act R-410A phasedown, and R-454B/R-32 affect repair quotes."
  structuredData={faqSchema}
>
  <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Calculator</a> / <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / <span>R-22 Refrigerant Phase-Out</span>
  </nav>

  <article class="prose">
    <h1>What the R-22 Phase-Out Means for Your Old HVAC System</h1>
    <p class="intro-lead">
      When a technician checks your outdoor condenser nameplate and sees <strong>R-22</strong> (legacy HCFC-22, often branded Freon), repair-vs-replace math shifts sharply. That is why our calculator runs a dedicated hard flag whenever a system exceeds 12 years old and uses R-22.
    </p>

    <h2>Verified Federal Refrigerant Timeline (U.S. EPA)</h2>
    <div class="source-callout">
      <ul style="margin-bottom: 0;">
        <li><strong>January 1, 2010:</strong> Under <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.epaR22} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. EPA Clean Air Act phaseout rules</a>, manufacturing and importing <strong>new</strong> air conditioners and heat pumps using R-22 was banned.</li>
        <li><strong>January 1, 2020:</strong> Virgin R-22 <strong>production and import</strong> ended completely in the United States.</li>
        <li><strong>2020–Present:</strong> Existing R-22 equipment can be repaired legally using <strong>recovered, recycled, or reclaimed R-22</strong> stock only.</li>
        <li><strong>AIM Act Transition (R-410A → R-454B / R-32):</strong> Under the <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.epaAim} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EPA American Innovation and Manufacturing (AIM) Act</a>, R-410A and other HFCs are undergoing gradual phasedown caps while new residential systems shift to <strong>R-454B</strong> and <strong>R-32</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <figure class="manual-figure">
      <img
        src="/images/fig-04-nameplate-refrigerant-timeline.svg"
        alt="Diagram highlighting MFR DATE and REFRIGERANT lines on a condenser nameplate alongside the EPA 2010, 2020 R-22 ban and AIM Act timeline."
        width="820"
        height="330"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Figure 04: Locate both MFR DATE and Refrigerant Type on the outdoor condenser rating plate before approving a recharge or coil swap.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Why R-22 Leaks Destroy Repair Economics</h2>
    <p>
      HVAC systems run as closed loops—they never consume refrigerant during normal operation. Low R-22 charge means an active copper leak in the coil, service valve, or line set. Repairing leaks on equipment built before 2010 creates three risks:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Reclaimed supply scarcity:</strong> Zero virgin supply means technicians draw strictly from reclaimed cylinders recovered from retired equipment, keeping per-pound prices elevated.</li>
      <li><strong>“Recharge-only” bids fail fast:</strong> Paying $600–$1,100 to top off R-22 without fixing the leak often loses that refrigerant before peak summer ends—see <a href="/guides/signs-your-hvac-is-dying/">terminal leaks vs. minor electrical fixes</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>No system-wide protection:</strong> Replacing a leaking coil on a 16-year-old outdoor compressor still leaves an old compressor with expired warranties and no <a href="/guides/seer2-explained/">SEER2 efficiency gains</a>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How to Verify What Refrigerant Your Unit Uses</h2>
    <p>
      Do not rely on hearsay or rough installation dates when a house has changed hands. Walk outside to the metal rating plate (data plate) riveted to the outdoor condenser cabinet and write down two printed fields before reading a repair quote:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Refrigerant field:</strong> It will explicitly print <code>R-22</code> (HCFC-22), <code>R-410A</code>, <code>R-32</code>, or <code>R-454B</code> along with factory weight in pounds and ounces. Also inspect the indoor air handler cabinet, as mismatched coils occasionally carry different labels.</li>
      <li><strong>Manufacture date (<code>MFR DATE</code>):</strong> Units built from 2010 onward were not factory-charged with R-22 under <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.epaR22} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">federal law</a> (except limited uncharged service replacements). Knowing the exact year tells you whether age clears Check C’s 12-year mark.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What About Alternative “Drop-In” R-22 Retrofits?</h2>
    <p>
      Some contractors propose recovering remaining R-22 and recharging an old system with an alternative refrigerant blend (such as R-407C, R-422B, or R-438A) instead of paying reclaimed R-22 prices. Before accepting a retrofit quote, weigh three engineering realities:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A retrofit still fails if the leak remains unfixed:</strong> Swapping refrigerants without replacing the corroded evaporator coil or leaking braze joint still loses the new charge.</li>
      <li><strong>Oil compatibility and capacity drop:</strong> R-22 compressors use mineral oil, whereas several replacement blends require changing compressor oil to POE or adjusting metering valves and can lower cooling capacity by several percent in hot weather.</li>
      <li><strong>Zero warranty reset on an aging compressor:</strong> A 16-year-old compressor still carries zero factory parts warranty after a chemical retrofit, so verify the total cost carefully inside the <a href="/guides/5000-rule/">calculator’s holding-cost sheet</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>R-22 vs. R-410A Under the AIM Act</h2>
    <p>
      Avoid confusing R-22’s completed 2020 production cutoff with the newer <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.epaAim} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AIM Act</a> HFC phasedown affecting R-410A. An 8-year-old R-410A unit needing a $450 capacitor or blower motor still has ample service supply and normally merits repair; only R-22 systems face zero virgin production.
    </p>

    <h2>When Replacing an R-22 Unit, Watch the Line Set</h2>
    <p>
      Always check <a href="/guides/how-to-read-hvac-quote/">your itemized replacement quote</a> to verify the copper line set is swapped—or flushed under manufacturer approval—because R-22 uses <strong>mineral oil</strong> whereas R-410A, R-454B, and R-32 require incompatible <strong>POE synthetic oil</strong>, and suction line diameters frequently differ.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked Example: R-22 Coil Leak vs. New System Math</h2>
    <div class="worked-example">
      <p>
        <strong>Scenario:</strong> Georgia homeowner, <strong>16-year-old R-22 split AC</strong> with a leaking evaporator coil, <strong>6-year</strong> planned stay, $400/yr expected repairs on aging parts, and $150/yr cooling savings:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Repair quote:</strong> Coil replacement + EPA 608 recovery + reclaimed R-22 = <strong>$2,450</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Replacement quote:</strong> Matched 14.3 SEER2 R-454B/R-32 unit + new copper line set = <strong>$9,600</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
        <strong>Audit check:</strong> Although $2,450 passes the <a href="/guides/5000-rule/">50% rule</a> (25.5%), Check A (<code>16 × $2,450 = $39,200</code>) and Check C (age &gt; 12 + R-22) both fire. Over 6 years, if the outdoor compressor fails later, cumulative spend reaches $2,450 + $9,600 = <strong>$12,050</strong> instead of replacing once cleanly.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-list">
      {FAQ_ITEMS.map((item) => (
        <details>
          <summary>{item.question}</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">{item.answer}</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>

    <h2>Run Your Exact Quotes</h2>
    <p>
      Test your unit’s age, refrigerant stamp, and stay horizon in our <a href="/">HVAC Repair or Replace Calculator</a>—especially if you plan to move within 1–2 years, when Check D holding costs can shift a non-leak repair toward Borderline.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
